<script>
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="Serve the app under a sub-path">
        <Code code={`APP_URL=https://example.com/crm abide start`} />

        <p>Set <code>APP_URL</code> and every page, handler and asset answers below its path.
            Nothing is rebuilt: <code>url</code>, a handler's address and every asset reference are
            resolved when they are read, never baked into the build. An address you write out by hand
            is not, which is one more reason to build them with <code>url</code>.</p>

        <p>A handler file answers at its own route, as <code>/companies/getCompany</code> does, so
            it moves below the path with the pages. What abide serves for itself is below
            <code>/__abide/**</code>: the client bundle, the health report, the OpenAPI document, the
            MCP endpoint, and a private handler.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Keep the CRM readable offline">
        <Code code={`ABIDE_OFFLINE=1 abide start`} />

        <p>Where <code>ABIDE_OFFLINE=1</code> turns it on, each page the CRM opens is kept on the
            device, with the answers its handlers gave, the public files it loaded and the bundle it
            runs. Off is the default, and a browser that kept the CRM while it was on drops what it
            kept on its next visit. A page opens from the device at once, however weak the signal,
            and the network is asked for the next copy behind it. So the visit after a deploy opens
            the CRM before it, and the visit after that the new one. A reload asks the network
            first.
            A handler's kept answer opens the same way. With the network gone, a page opened in the
            last year loads again with its data and works. One never opened says the device is
            offline, and a link to it leaves the page you are on standing.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the CRM</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>cache-control: private, stale-while-revalidate=604800, stale-if-error=31536000</code></td><td>what a handler's answer carries, so the device shows what it kept while it asks again</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>cache-control: private, max-age=0, stale-if-error=31536000</code></td><td>what a page carries, so the browser's own cache never opens it stale and what the device kept stands in offline</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>cache-control: public, max-age=0, stale-if-error=31536000</code></td><td>the same, for a public file</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>clear-site-data: "cache"</code></td><td>what a sign-in or a sign-out answers, so the browser forgets what it kept for the last person</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>What is kept is what those headers allow, and nothing else decides it. A handler that
            writes <code>cache-control: no-store</code> through <code>response</code> is never kept,
            and an answer with a <code>ttl</code> is shown from the device until its
            <code>max-age</code> runs out. A refresh always asks the network.</p>

        <p>With <code>src/public/icon.png</code> beside it, each page also links a manifest, so a
            phone can add the CRM to its home screen, which is what keeps what was saved there for
            good.</p>

        <p>A kept page shows what it showed when it was last opened, live updates stop, and a
            change fails until the network is back. A sign-in, a sign-out or a submitted form clears
            what was kept, and a new build replaces it. <code>abide dev</code> keeps nothing.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
